Q5 – Derive a Generic Ranking Component from a Principle (7 Marks)
1. Problem Analysis
The objective is to develop a reusable Java ranking component for teams, players, colleges and quiz teams.

The sports leaderboard follows three ranking conditions:
1. Points – Higher points rank first.
2. Goal Difference – If points are equal, higher goal difference ranks first.
3. Team Name – If both points and goal difference are equal, alphabetical order (A–Z) is followed.
Selected approach: Comparator

I selected Comparator instead of Comparable because different objects may require different ranking rules. A separate comparator makes the ranking logic reusable without modifying the original class.
2. Design Approach
- Team stores team name, points and goal difference.
- Comparator defines the ranking rules.
- Leaderboard<T> stores objects using a generic type.
- ranking() creates a new sorted list without modifying the original list.
- List<? extends T> is useful when adding data from a list of T or its subclasses.
Ranking design:
Team Data

Name, Points, Goal Difference

Comparator

Points → Goal Difference → Name

**Generic Leaderboard
Final Sorted Ranking


3. Pseudocode
START

Create a comparator:
    Compare points in descending order
    If points are equal:
        Compare goal difference in descending order
    If goal difference is equal:
        Compare names in alphabetical order

Create Leaderboard
Add team details

FUNCTION ranking():
    Create a copy of the original list
    Sort the copied list using comparator
    Return the sorted list

Display ranking

STOP


4. Java Source Code

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Collections;

class Team {
    final String name;
    final int points;
    final int goalDiff;

    Team(String name, int points, int goalDiff) {
        this.name = name;
        this.points = points;
        this.goalDiff = goalDiff;
    }

    public String toString() {
        return name + "(" + points + "," + goalDiff + ")";
    }
}

class Leaderboard<T> {
    private final List<T> items = new ArrayList<>();
    private final Comparator<? super T> order;

    Leaderboard(Comparator<? super T> order) {
        this.order = order;
    }

    void add(T item) {
        items.add(item);
    }

    // Return a sorted copy
    List<T> ranking() {
        List<T> result = new ArrayList<>(items);
        result.sort(order);
        return result;
    }
}

public class LeaderboardDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Comparator<Team> byRank =
            Comparator.comparingInt((Team t) -> t.points)
            .reversed()
            .thenComparing(
                Comparator.comparingInt(
                    (Team t) -> t.goalDiff
                ).reversed()
            )
            .thenComparing(t -> t.name);

        Leaderboard<Team> board =
            new Leaderboard<>(byRank);

        board.add(new Team("Panthers", 9, 4));
        board.add(new Team("Eagles", 9, 6));
        board.add(new Team("Cobras", 6, 1));
        board.add(new Team("Bisons", 9, 4));

        System.out.println("Team Rankings:");
        System.out.println(board.ranking());
    }
}



