Faulty comparator example:
return b.points - a.points;


This subtraction-based comparison can overflow for extreme integer values. It should be replaced with safe comparison methods such as Comparator.comparingInt() and reversed().

A comparator that checks only points is also faulty for this problem because it ignores the goal-difference and alphabetical tie-breakers.
8. Correctness Argument
The comparator follows a consistent order:
- First, it compares points in descending order.
- If points are equal, it compares goal difference in descending order.
- If both are equal, it compares names in ascending alphabetical order.
- If all three fields are equal, it returns zero.
Each comparison is consistent and transitive. Combining these comparisons using thenComparing() preserves the ordering principle.

Therefore, if A ranks above B and B ranks above C, A also ranks above C. Equal items compare as equal.
9. Alternative Concepts and Complexity Analysis
Approach	Time complexity	Extra space
Comparator chain with one sort	O(n log n)	O(n) for copied list
Comparable with one sort	O(n log n)	O(n) for copied list
Three stable sorts	O(3n log n) = O(n log n)	Depends on sorting implementation

Comparable: The Team class can implement Comparable<Team> and define its natural ordering using compareTo(). However, it makes one ranking rule the default for that class.

Three stable sorts: Sort by name first, then goal difference, and finally points. Stable sorting preserves the previous ordering when the next key is equal. This requires three sorting operations.

Best approach: Comparator chaining is easier to reuse for players, colleges and quiz teams because each type can have its own comparator without changing the generic Leaderboard<T> class.
10. Result
Thus, a generic Java leaderboard was developed successfully using Comparator, thenComparing() and Leaderboard<T>. The program ranks teams according to points, goal difference and alphabetical order. The same generic component can be reused for other ranking applications.

Q6 – Priority, Order and Fairness in a Queue (8 Marks)
1. Problem Analysis
The objective is to develop a hospital emergency triage system using Java PriorityQueue.

Patients are treated according to two conditions:
1. Severity level – Patients with severity 1 are treated first, followed by 2, 3, 4 and 5.
2. Arrival order – If two patients have the same severity, the patient who arrived first must be treated first.
Selected approach: PriorityQueue with Comparator

A normal FIFO queue is not sufficient because it treats patients only according to arrival order. It cannot automatically select the most critical patient.

A PriorityQueue with a suitable comparator can arrange patients according to severity and arrival order.
2. Design Approach
Each patient contains:
- Patient name
- Severity level
- Arrival order (sequence number)
The comparator checks severity first and arrival order second.
Patient Arrival

Name, Severity, Arrival Number

PriorityQueue

Severity: 1 → 5

Same severity: First arrival first

Treatment Order

Most severe patient treated first


3. Pseudocode
START

Create Patient class
    Store name, severity and arrival number

Create PriorityQueue
    Compare severity in ascending order
    If severity is equal:
        Compare arrival number in ascending order

Add 8 patients to the queue

Display waiting list:
    Copy the priority queue
    Remove patients from the copy one by one
    Display treatment order

Treatment:
    Remove the highest priority patient
    Display the treated patient

STOP


4. Java Source Code

import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;

class Patient {
    String name;
    int severity;
    int arrival;

    Patient(String name, int severity, int arrival) {
        this.name = name;
        this.severity = severity;
        this.arrival = arrival;
    }

    public String toString() {
        return name + " (Severity: " + severity +
               ", Arrival: " + arrival + ")";
    }
}

public class HospitalTriage {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Comparator<Patient> order =
            Comparator.comparingInt((Patient p) -> p.severity)
            .thenComparingInt(p -> p.arrival);

        PriorityQueue<Patient> queue =
            new PriorityQueue<>(order);

        // Adding 8 patients
        queue.add(new Patient("Arun", 3, 1));
        queue.add(new Patient("Bala", 1, 2));
        queue.add(new Patient("Charan", 2, 3));
        queue.add(new Patient("David", 1, 4));
        queue.add(new Patient("Ezhil", 4, 5));
        queue.add(new Patient("Farhan", 2, 6));
        queue.add(new Patient("Gokul", 3, 7));
        queue.add(new Patient("Hari", 1, 8));

        System.out.println("Waiting List:");

        // Display in priority order without
        // changing the original queue
        PriorityQueue<Patient> copy =
            new PriorityQueue<>(queue);

        while (!copy.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println(copy.poll());
        }

        System.out.println("\nTreatment Order:");

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            Patient p = queue.poll();
            System.out.println("Treating: " + p);
        }
    }
}



